    <briefdescription>
<para>Nodes in the <ref refid="classllvm_1_1anonymous__namespace_02_a_m_d_g_p_u_split_module_8cpp_03_1_1_split_graph" kindref="compound">SplitGraph</ref> contain both incoming, and outgoing edges. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para><ref refid="structllvm_1_1_incoming" kindref="compound">Incoming</ref> edges have this node as their Dst, and Outgoing ones have this node as their Src.</para>
<para><ref refid="structllvm_1_1anonymous__namespace_02_a_m_d_g_p_u_split_module_8cpp_03_1_1_split_graph_1_1_edge" kindref="compound">Edge</ref> objects are shared by both nodes in Src/Dst. They provide immediate feedback on how two nodes are related, and in which direction they are related, which is valuable information to make splitting decisions.</para>
<para>Nodes are fundamentally abstract, and any consumers of the graph should treat them as such. While a node will be a function most of the time, we could also create nodes for any other reason. In the future, we could have single nodes for multiple functions, or nodes for GVs, etc. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
